-- title:
--   Existence of a Jacobian pack over an algebraically closed field
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, $K$ algebraically closed, and assume $F$ is a one-variable function field over $K$ in the sense that there is $x \in F$ transcendental over $K$ with $F$ finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $x$. Assume further that $F$ is a curve over $K$ in the project's sense: every nonzero $f \in F$ has a divisor, i.e. a finitely supported integer-valued function on the places of $F/K$ whose value at each place $v$ is $\mathrm{ord}_v(f)$ and whose degree is $0$; the residue field of every place is a finite $K$-module; and the module of Kähler differentials $\Omega[F/K]$ is free of rank $1$ over $F$. The conclusion asserts the existence of a scheme $J$ and a morphism $c : J \to \operatorname{Spec} K$ which is smooth and proper, with $J$ connected as a topological space, together with a morphism $\mathrm{mul}$ from the fibre product of $c$ with itself to $J$ and a bijection $\mathrm{pts}$ between $\mathrm{Pic}^0(K,F)$ — the group of degree-zero divisors modulo principal divisors — and the set of sections $\sigma : \operatorname{Spec} K \to J$ of $c$, such that for all $x, y$ the section $\mathrm{pts}(x+y)$ equals the morphism induced into the fibre product by $\mathrm{pts}(x)$ and $\mathrm{pts}(y)$ followed by $\mathrm{mul}$. Only this compatibility of $\mathrm{mul}$ with addition on $K$-points is asserted; no group-scheme axioms for $\mathrm{mul}$ are claimed.
--
--   This is the existence of the Jacobian of a curve over an algebraically closed field, in any characteristic, packaged as the data needed downstream: a smooth proper connected $K$-scheme whose $K$-points carry the degree-zero divisor class group additively. It feeds the divisibility statements about $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ used further on, via [`AlgebraicCurve.Pic0.exists_zsmul_eq_of_finiteDimensional_ratFunc_of_forall_pow_eq_self`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.Pic0.exists_zsmul_eq_of_finiteDimensional_ratFunc_of_forall_pow_eq_self).
